## 6. Existing 400 / validation patterns

Two coexisting patterns for returning 400:

**(a) Declarative bean-validation** — `ValidateOwner` binds `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto`; constraint violations throw `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, handled by **`ValidationExceptionHandler`** (`.../escalation/ValidationExceptionHandler.java`) which returns a `ProblemDetail` (400) with a top-level `errors` array of field names plus `schemaValidationErrors`. Uses `ProblemDetails.build(...)`.

**(b) Imperative "Require/Normalize" step throws a checked escalation → handler returns 400** — this is the pattern to copy for the disposable-domain rule. Canonical example, `InvalidEmailException` + `InvalidEmailExceptionHandler`:
```java
public class InvalidEmailException extends Exception {
    private final String email;
    public InvalidEmailException(String email) {
        super("Email must be a syntactically valid address when present, but was: '" + email + "'");
        this.email = email;
    }
    public String getEmail() { return this.email; }
}
```
```java
public class InvalidEmailExceptionHandler {
    public void handle(@Parameter InvalidEmailException ex,
            ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>>> response) {
        response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
                .body(Map.of("errors", List.of("email"))));
    }
}
```
The same shape is used by `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`, `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` (both 400, body `{"errors":[...]}`), `PostcodeOutOfRangeExceptionHandler`. Non-400 variants: `DuplicateIdentityExceptionHandler` (409), `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`/`DailyOwnerLimitExceededExceptionHandler` (429), `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`.

### Naming conventions (from AGENTS.md, confirmed in code)
Verb + entity, one public method (`service`/`handle`) per class: `Load<E>`, `Build<E>`, `Validate<E>`, `Apply<E>`, `Save<E>`, `Delete<E>`, `RespondWith<E>`/`...Created`/`...Updated`, `RespondWithNoContent`. Business-rule guards are named **`Require<Rule>`** (e.g. `RequireOwnerFields`, `RequirePostcodeForCity`, `RequireUniqueIdentity`, `RequireCityCapacity`, `RequireDailyOwnerLimit`, `RequireRegistrationDateNotFuture`) and throw a checked escalation. Normalizers are `Normalize<E><Field>`. Shared logic goes in a package-private helper (e.g. `EmailNormalizer`) because each function class may expose only one public method.

### Suggested implementation shape (for your planning)
A new `Require`-style step after the existing `email` step in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (e.g. `RequireEmailNotDisposable` reading `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`), a checked exception (e.g. `DisposableEmailDomainException extends Exception`), its handler returning 400 with `{"errors":["email"]}`, and an escalation YAML `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DisposableEmailDomainException.yml`. The blocklist domain set can live in a package-private helper (mirroring `EmailNormalizer`), and must include `mailinator.com` to satisfy the existing test. Blank emails must remain allowed (consistent with `NormalizeOwnerEmail`).

